{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"jq -c 'if has(\\\"claim_id\\\") then {claim_id,source_id,text,evidence_excerpts} else empty end' verification/example_site/spaces/compilers/claims/*.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"jq -c 'if has(\\\"claim_id\\\") then {claim_id,source_id,text,evidence_excerpts} else empty end' verification/example_site/spaces/compilers/claims/*.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"claim_id\":\"claim-classic-scalar-optimizations-can-be-reformulated-to-operate-on-super-blocks-and--79ce55378df0\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Classic scalar optimizations can be reformulated to operate on super-blocks and super-block loops rather than only on traditional basic blocks or whole loops.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Table 2 shows a list of classic code optimizations that we have extended to use profile information.\",\"The non-loop based code optimizations work on a single super-block at a time. The loop based code optimizations work on a single super-block loop at a time.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-direct-prediction-of-the-best-compiler-option-is-practical-for-relatively-small--fe68da808ef3\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Direct prediction of the best compiler option is practical for relatively small optimization problems and has produced measurable gains in loop unrolling and thread-coarsening studies.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Stephenson and Amarasinghe ... used over 2,500 loops from 72 benchmarks to train two machine learning models ... their techniques correctly predict the unroll factor for 65% of the testing loops, leading to on average, a 5% improvement for the SPEC 2000 benchmark suite.\",\"These papers show that supervised machine learning can be a powerful tool for modelling problems with a relatively small number of optimisation options.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-execution-profiles-integrated-into-an-optimizing-c-compiler-can-guide-classic-op--20be52b7603e\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Execution profiles integrated into an optimizing C compiler can guide classic optimizations toward the program regions that run most often.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"An execution profiler can be integrated into an optimizing compiler to provide the compiler with run-time information about user programs. For each code optimization, the profile information along with information from static loop analysis is used to reduce the execution time of the most frequently executed program regions.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-feature-selection-and-dimension-reduction-are-necessary-in-compiler-learning-pro--ecd12a4a4f1f\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Feature selection and dimension reduction are necessary in compiler-learning problems because larger feature spaces require more training examples and can contain redundant information.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"As the number of features increase so does the number of training examples needed to build an accurate model. Hence, we need to limit the dimension of the feature space. In compiler research, commonly, an initial large, high dimensional candidate feature space is pruned via feature selection, or projected into a lower dimensional space.\",\"Principal Component Analysis ... is applied to map the 17 candidate features into 7 aggregated features, so that each aggregated feature is a linear combination of the original features.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-future-research-in-machine-learning-compilation-extends-beyond-choosing-optimiza--faf587f1fc17\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Future research in machine-learning compilation extends beyond choosing optimizations to mapping program and hardware spaces, learning analyses, discovering common features, and possibly learning better intermediate representations.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"The open research directions go beyond predicting the best optimisations to apply. One central issue is what does the program space look like?\",\"Can machine learning also be applied to compiler analysis? For instance is it possible to learn dataflow or point-to analysis? ... Can we learn the ideal compiler intermediate representation?\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-make-compiler-optimization-more-evidence--20aeeff3a32c\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Machine-learning-based compilation can make compiler optimization more evidence based because models must be grounded in empirical data that can be tested and independently scrutinized.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"One of the real benefits of machine learning based approaches is that it forces an empirical driven approach to compiler construction. New models have to be based on empirical data which can then be verified by independent experimentation.\",\"This paper has introduced machine learning based compilation and described its power in determining an evidence based approach to compiler optimisation.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-machine-learning-based-compilation-can-use-a-model-inside-the-compiler-to-choose--f8131a5c483d\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Machine-learning-based compilation can use a model inside the compiler to choose compiler heuristics or optimizations for a given program according to learned evidence about what makes code faster, smaller, or less power hungry.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Given a program, compiler writers would like to know what compiler heuristic or optimisation to apply in order to make the code better. Better often means execute faster, but can also mean smaller code footprint or reduced power. Machine learning can be used to build a model used within the compiler, that makes such decisions for any given program.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-machine-learning-based-compiler-optimization-is-limited-by-the-quality-represent--f9c7d385cc26\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Machine-learning-based compiler optimization is limited by the quality, representativeness, and cost of its training data and cannot by itself create legal transformations or derive legality analyses.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Machine learning can only learn form what is provided by the compiler writer. Machine learning can not invent new program transformations to apply nor can it derive analysis that determines whether or not a transformation is legal – all of this is beyond its scope.\",\"The most immediate problem continues to be gathering enough sufficient high quality training data.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-on-the-benchmark-suite-profile-based-super-block-optimizations-averaged-a-1-15-s--e5d7486c0e56\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"On the benchmark suite, profile-based super-block optimizations averaged a 1.15 speedup over the prototype compiler's globally optimized code.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"name global profile local MIPS. 04 GNU.O ... cmp 1.0 1.42 0.95 0.96 0.95 ... wc 1.0 1.32 0.97 0.96 0.87 ... avg. 1.0 1.15 0.89 0.96 0.88 ... Table 5: Speed for each individual benchmark.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-profile-based-classic-optimizations-are-intended-to-complement-conventional-opti--6bcefbcf9607\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Profile-based classic optimizations are intended to complement conventional optimizations rather than replace them.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"It should be noted that profile-based code optimizations are not alternatives to conventional optimizations, but are meant to be applied in addition to conventional optimizations.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-program-features-provide-the-quantifiable-representation-that-allows-learning-al--a254f7b701be\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Program features provide the quantifiable representation that allows learning algorithms to reason about source code, intermediate representation, runtime profiles, or combinations of those properties.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Machine learning relies on a set of quantifiable properties, or features, to characterise the programs. There are many different features that can be used. These include the static data structures extracted from the program source code or the compiler intermediate representation ... dynamic profiling information ... or a combination of the both.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-super-blocks-represent-frequently-executed-paths-as-linear-basic-block-sequences--7bbb510c9bb0\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Super-blocks represent frequently executed paths as linear basic-block sequences that can only be entered at the first block but may exit from any block.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"A super-block has the following features. (1) It is a linear sequence of basic blocks B(i),i = l..n, where n > 1. (2) It can be entered only from B( 1). (3) The program control may leave the super-block from any basic block.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-supervised-learning-for-compiler-optimization-learns-correlations-between-progra--217abf25c992\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Supervised learning for compiler optimization learns correlations between program-feature values and the optimization decisions that produce optimal or near-optimal empirical performance.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Using supervised machine learning, a predictive model is trained on empirical performance data (labelled outputs) and important quantifiable properties (features) of representative programs. The model learns the correlation between these feature values and the optimisation decision that delivers the optimal (or nearly optimal) performance.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-tail-duplication-creates-additional-optimization-opportunities-because-it-can-ma--6c81300e0307\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Tail duplication creates additional optimization opportunities because it can make an earlier operation the sole producer of operands used later in a hot path.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Duplicating the tail part of a trace to form a super-block eliminates control paths that enter the trace in the middle. For many op(x) and op(y) instruction pairs, op(x) becomes the sole producer of one or more source operands of op(y). Therefore, profile-based operation combining can find more opportunities for optimization than traditional operation combining.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-the-experiments-used-separate-profiling-inputs-and-performance-measurement-input--ee5ac2fa5a94\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"The experiments used separate profiling inputs and performance-measurement inputs across realistic C application programs.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"For each benchmark program, we have selected a number of input data for profiling. Table 4 shows the inputs that are used for each benchmark program. For each benchmark program, we have selected one additional input and used that input to measure the performance.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-the-implementation-cost-of-profile-based-classic-optimization-was-modest-relativ--062df22ec16c\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"The implementation cost of profile-based classic optimization was modest relative to the existing global optimizer.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"The global code optimizer in our prototype compiler consists of approximately 32,000 lines of C code. The profile-based classic code optimizer consists of approximately 11,000 lines of C code. The profiler has about 2,000 lines of C code and a few assembly language subroutines.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-the-machine-learning-compiler-workflow-separates-model-learning-from-deployment--a04ee1ab9738\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"The machine-learning compiler workflow separates model learning from deployment: training data is used to learn a model, and the learned model is then inserted into the compiler to make predictions for new programs.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"There are two main stages involved: learning and deployment. The first stage learns the model based on training data, while the second uses the model on new unseen programs.\",\"In the final step, the learned model is inserted into the compiler to predict the best optimisation decisions for new programs.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-the-major-effectiveness-mechanisms-are-eliminating-entries-into-the-middle-of-tr--4bc0ff5e21b0\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"The major effectiveness mechanisms are eliminating entries into the middle of traces and optimizing the most frequently executed loop path.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"We have identified two major reasons why these code optimizations are effective: (1) eliminating control flows into the middle sections of a trace, and (2) optimizing the most frequently executed path in a loop.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-the-profile-based-transformations-controlled-executable-size-growth-averaging-1--5bacab4b8a28\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"The profile-based transformations controlled executable size growth, averaging 1.07 relative size compared with globally optimized programs.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"For these experiments we use an execution count threshold of 100. Table 6 shows how code optimizations affect the sizes of the benchmark programs.\",\"name global profile local ... xlisp 1.0 1.20 1.01 ... avg. 1.0 1.07 1.00 ... Table 6: Ratios of code expansion.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-thread-coarsening-decisions-are-context-dependent-because-coarsening-can-improve--4878860bda89\",\"source_id\":\"source-02-machine-learning-in-compiler-optimization--5fbaa9c3f69d\",\"text\":\"Thread coarsening decisions are context dependent because coarsening can improve instruction-level parallelism and memory behavior while also reducing parallelism or increasing register pressure on particular programs and hardware.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"Thread coarsening can improve performance through increasing instruction-level parallelism, reducing the number of memory-access operations, and eliminating redundant computation when the same value is computed in every work-item. However, it can also have several negative side-effects such as reducing the total amount of parallelism and increasing the register pressure, which can lead to slowdown performance.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-trace-selection-followed-by-tail-duplication-converts-frequently-executed-traces--1483ee9d0534\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Trace selection followed by tail duplication converts frequently executed traces into super-blocks by removing control-flow entries into the middle of a trace.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"The formation of super-blocks is a two step procedure: (1) trace selection and (2) tail duplication. Trace selection identifies basic blocks that tend to execute in a sequence and groups them into a trace.\",\"After trace selection, each trace is converted into a super-block by duplicating the tail part of the trace, in order to ensure that the program control can only enter at the top basic block.\"]}\n{\"claim_id\":\"claim-weighted-flow-graphs-expose-hot-paths-by-attaching-execution-counts-to-basic-blo--49c47f8431d5\",\"source_id\":\"source-01-using-profile-information-to-assist-classic-code-optimizations--219edcf6f0cf\",\"text\":\"Weighted flow graphs expose hot paths by attaching execution counts to basic blocks and taken counts to control-flow arcs.\",\"evidence_excerpts\":[\"We define a weighted flow graph as a quadruplet {V,E, count, arc.count], where each node in V is a basic block, each arc in E is a potential control flow path between two basic blocks, count{v) is a function that returns the execution count of a basic block u, and arc-count(e) is a function that returns the taken count of a control flow path e.\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"reasoning","text":"**Working on JSON schema**\n\nI need to write JSON directly, focusing on the section schema. It states that there should be a sections array, but validation requires each section to be an object with at least one non-empty body. To be safe, I think each section should follow the shape: {\"title\": \"...\", \"body\": \"...\"}. The schema isn't clear about the title requirement, but it emphasizes the need for the body field. I also need to consider the topic_id, which seems optional but shouldn't include any invented canonical IDs.\n**Organizing source IDs in topics**\n\nI need to ensure every topic includes exactly two source IDs, with one being the ingested source ID. Each topic may be concept-centric, so I should verify that the \"must_include_claim_ids_from_ingested_source\" rule applies across topics. I can list all ten claim IDs across topics, and I should check if each is included at least once. After confirming their presence in various topics, I’ll also need to generate JSON with four topics, each containing an array of section objects. Maybe I could omit the topic_id strings to avoid creating invented IDs, but I have to keep the JSON strict.\n**Writing JSON with shell commands**\n\nI need to write a JSON object to a file using a single shell command with a heredoc. I should create the directory structure if it doesn't exist and overwrite the output JSON path without checking if it already exists. Using `mkdir -p` alongside `cat > ... <<'JSON'` helps achieve this. While I could use `jq` to validate after writing, the user only wants one JSON object and doesn’t require validation, so I can skip that step to avoid unnecessary probing."}}
